<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n49953" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ūsŭālis</span></b> ,
		e, adj.
		2. usus. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>That is for use</i>, <i>fit for
			use</i> (post-class.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">mancipia,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Dig. 39, 4, 16, § 3</span></i>
			.—* </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>Usual</i>, <i>common</i>, <i>ordinary</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">sermo,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Sid. Ep. 4, 10</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">Ambros. in Luc. 7</span></i>, n.
			150.—Hence, adv.: <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ūsŭālĭter</span></b> , <i>in the usual manner</i> (late Lat.),
					<i><span class="en">Cassiod. Var. 2, 39</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
